<v NARRATOR>This drama uses an AI-generated voice. Voiced via Gemini 2.5.

3
00:00:15.861 --> 00:00:23.552
<v NARRATOR>Clip is a small, neat sparrow with a tiny pair of silver scissors tucked under one wing.

4
00:00:23.552 --> 00:00:33.283
<v NARRATOR>Patter the writing coach started to greet her — good morning, how are you, lovely day, isn't it — and her scissors flashed.

5
00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:35.974
<v CLIP>*Snip, snip, snip.*

6
00:00:35.974 --> 00:00:45.985
<v NARRATOR>The dead words fell away like trimmed thread. What was left hanging in the air was just: "Lovely day."

7
00:00:45.985 --> 00:00:58.356
<v CLIP>I cut the parts that weren't doing anything. My name is Clip. I trim the filler out of talk. Hello, how are you, I'm fine — that's just throat-clearing. The talk is what comes *after*.

8
00:00:58.356 --> 00:01:08.927
<v NARRATOR>A long, draggy conversation floated past — full of "so anyway," and "you know," and "what was I saying?"

9
00:01:08.927 --> 00:01:20.538
<v CLIP>*Snip. Snip.* It's the same talk. Just without the dead weight. Shorter. Sharper. Every word that's left actually *does* something.

10
00:01:20.538 --> 00:01:32.589
<v NARRATOR>Then she showed her favorite trick — a scene that began with a character waking, eating breakfast, walking, knocking, saying hello.

11
00:01:32.589 --> 00:01:41.960
<v CLIP>Start late. Don't begin a scene at hello. Begin it where it gets *interesting.* Cut everything before that.

12
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:51.691
<v NARRATOR>Patter felt the click — his young writers wrote every "hi" and "bye," and their scenes took a whole page to get going.

13
00:01:51.691 --> 00:02:03.222
<v CLIP>Read your dialogue out loud and find the words that aren't doing anything. The hellos. The ums. Then snip them.

14
00:02:03.222 --> 00:02:17.913
<v CLIP>But careful — economy doesn't mean *cold.* Sometimes a hello matters, if it's awkward, or the first word two enemies have said in years. Keep the words that *do* something.

15
00:02:17.913 --> 00:02:22.244
<v NARRATOR>A young writer asked how to know if a line was working.

16
00:02:22.244 --> 00:02:34.295
<v CLIP>Take it out. If the talk still makes sense and feels the same, the line wasn't working. If something important breaks, put it back — it was earning its place.

17
00:02:34.295 --> 00:02:41.026
<v NARRATOR>After the workshop, Patter asked if cutting so much ever felt like loss.

18
00:02:41.026 --> 00:02:53.877
<v CLIP>It used to. But then I noticed how a talk *breathes* once the dead words are gone. Cutting isn't loss. It's making room — so the part that matters gets to be heard at last.

